<section>
    <h2>Tests can talk HTTP too</h2>
    <p>
        Not everything worth testing has a button. Playwright can send requests straight from the test: to set data up
        before the page opens, to check an API on its own, or to read what the UI just saved. <code>Page.APIRequest</code>
        shares the page's cookies, so a signed-in page makes signed-in requests.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Sending a request</h2>
<pre><code>var reply = await Page.APIRequest.PostAsync("/api/orders", new()
{
    DataObject = new { item = "lantern", quantity = 2 },          // sent as JSON
    Headers = new Dictionary&lt;string, string&gt; { ["X-Api-Key"] = key },
});

Assert.That(reply.Status, Is.EqualTo(201));                     // nothing throws on a 404 or 500
var location = reply.Headers["location"];                         // header names are lower case
var order = await (await Page.APIRequest.GetAsync(location)).JsonAsync();
Assert.That(order?.GetProperty("item").GetString(), Is.EqualTo("lantern"));</code></pre>
    <p class="tip">
        <strong>Always check the status.</strong> An error reply is still a reply, so a request that failed looks
        exactly like one that worked until you assert on it.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Without a page</h2>
    <p>For a pure API test, make a request context of its own, with a base URL and headers every request shares:</p>
<pre><code>await using var api = await Playwright.APIRequest.NewContextAsync(new()
{
    BaseURL = "https://shop.example",
    ExtraHTTPHeaders = new Dictionary&lt;string, string&gt; { ["X-Api-Key"] = key },
});
var reply = await api.GetAsync("/api/orders/42");
await Expect(reply).ToBeOKAsync();</code></pre>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The Oracle's Well</h2>
    <p>
        The Oracle has no buttons. Read the notice by the well, POST a question with your offering in the
        <code>X-Offering</code> header, follow the reply to where the answer is kept, and speak the prophecy on the
        page. Ten seconds.
    </p>
</section>
